WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.465
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.471 --> 00:00:07.470
ZOLTAN ARATO: All right.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.700
Good morning, everyone.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.360
My name is Zoltan Arato.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.060
I'm a product manager
of Google Cloud IoT.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:18.380
Let me also introduce my
co-presenters for today--

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:24.950
Kerry Maletsky and Lucio di
Jasio, both from Microchip.

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:25.470
All right.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:28.380
IoT Security-- Solving
the Primary Hurdle

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.840
to IoT Deployment.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.759
In this breakout
session, we are going

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:37.680
to show you evidence why
connective product companies

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:44.100
and IoT fleet operators find
security a top challenge.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:49.620
We will also talk about how and
why these challenges ultimately

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:54.320
come down to a question
of cost and complexity.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.442
And most importantly, we
will show you a toolset

00:00:57.442 --> 00:00:58.400
or give you a toolset--

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:02.270
an end-to-end toolset-- that
can help you solving these

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:04.930
challenges.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:07.420
So let's take a
look at our agenda.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.410
First, very quickly, we
will touch up on the why--

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:14.580
why the need is undeniable,
why it's a top concern.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:18.990
Then we are going to talk about
the theory, the principles

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:22.440
of solving IoT security.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:25.140
And probably the longest
part of the presentation

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:32.560
is about the solution proposed
by Google and Microchip.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:34.750
So let's start by this number.

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.520
This is an actual
Gartner number,

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.890
so this is not an estimation.

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:43.120
And this is from last year--
number of connected devices--

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.310
so it's probably more now.

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:48.160
I guess all of you
remember those estimations

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:51.250
from a few years back
predicting super-high numbers

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:52.550
of connected devices.

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:54.460
Now this is real.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:02:00.070
This is happening, our devices
generating more data than ever,

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:02.680
and that also means
that the attacks

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:05.770
surface is growing rapidly.

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:11.080
And important to highlight
that these devices are, most

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:12.700
of the time,
resource constrained,

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:15.010
and security
measurements are close

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:18.160
to nonexistent many times.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.260
And there is evidence to that.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:25.690
So we see a clear
acceleration of attacks.

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:28.240
Probably the most
well known is Mirai.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:31.960
I'm sure many of you
have heard about Mirai.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:37.960
It was a botnet
attack two years ago.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:41.960
It hit low-security cameras--

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:48.010
CCTV cameras-- and
performed a DDoS attack

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:51.280
against [INAUDIBLE],, one
of the biggest domain name

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.070
services in the US and
brought the internet down,

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:56.620
or most of the internet
down, for multiple hours.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:02.990
Just another example,
which happened this year--

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:09.620
a hacker got into a casino using
a thermostat of a fish tank.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:15.530
So not only DDoS attacks
are a threat in relation

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:19.070
to IoT deployments,
but also, these devices

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:21.830
are connected to
your network, so they

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:23.780
can access their network.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.570
So if a hacker can
get to your device,

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:27.860
they can go into your network.

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:36.220
What this really means to us is
that IoT security is complex.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:38.240
It's challenging.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:42.520
It takes time to secure
an IoT deployment,

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:47.290
and it's not something you can
do once your fleet is deployed.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:49.750
It cannot be an afterthought.

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:52.210
You really need to
take care of security

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:53.560
before you deploy devices.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:00.830
So let's take a look at the
challenges of IoT security.

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:06.470
This is just a summary.

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:09.440
We are going more into
the details very soon.

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:12.360
So strong device identity
and authentication,

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.680
how you identify
your devices, how you

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:20.120
authenticate them, how you
use asymmetric keys for these,

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:25.280
securing the manufacturing
line, how you transfer keys--

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.290
it's a complex process.

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:30.170
How you protect
your keys and how

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:33.620
you make sure that
the software ran

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:37.700
by your device is what
it is supposed to run--

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:42.050
and key rotation-- how to
treat keys as ephemeral

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:46.580
and how to update
keys as needed.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:50.030
And of course, application
layer security--

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:51.770
it's very important,
but we are not

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:54.590
going to talk about
application layer security.

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:57.650
We are going to focus on
the IoT-related parts.

00:04:57.650 --> 00:04:59.480
It's not that it's not easy--

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:04.250
or it's not that it's easy, but
it's much more well-described

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:07.570
or discussed, so we are not
going to talk about that here.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:13.680
Even though most
of these principles

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:17.810
can be implemented
using software only,

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:21.000
a much higher level
of security can

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:25.480
be achieved if we involve
specialized hardware.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:30.260
So that's why hardware plays
a key role in secure IoT

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:35.520
deployments, and that's also
why we partnered with Microchip,

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:43.020
because they're offering
plays a key part in what we

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:49.330
offer as a secure solution
for end-to-end deployment.

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:51.270
So I would like to invite
Kerry on stage, who

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:53.296
will talk more about Microchip.

00:05:57.552 --> 00:05:59.510
KERRY MALETSKY: So I
don't know how many of you

00:05:59.510 --> 00:06:00.980
are familiar with Microchip.

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:04.640
We've been around
for 30 years, and we

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:08.210
ship over $2 billion
8-bit MCUs every year,

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:10.070
so maybe many of
you out there have

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:12.290
designed some kind of
system, whether PIC or an AVR

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:16.452
microcontroller, and you're
familiar with Microchip.

00:06:16.452 --> 00:06:18.410
You may or may not be
familiar with the broader

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:21.800
range of products that Microchip
has, among other things

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:25.220
like wireless and wired
connectivity solutions

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:29.510
and analog devices, interface
chips, and security devices.

00:06:29.510 --> 00:06:31.580
So all in all, that
kind of gives us

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:36.110
this idea of having devices
that are smart like an MCU,

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:38.030
connected wireless,
which is really

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:40.010
what IoT is all
about, and secure,

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:42.620
which we're going to
make the case here

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:47.210
is a key part of how you
build an IoT endpoint--

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:49.430
thinking about security
at a hardware level.

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:52.370
And obviously, Microchip
has a good solution

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:54.930
across that board.

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:57.880
So let me just start and kind
of reiterate some of the points

00:06:57.880 --> 00:06:59.770
that Zoltan made.

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:04.650
It's hard to overestimate
the importance of a device

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:06.780
identity, right?

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:09.270
When you get past all the
three- and four-letter acronyms,

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:10.560
you get all the words.

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:13.380
Everybody is all happy about
what they got in security.

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:16.140
You have an identity
at an endpoint.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:16.979
It's a number.

00:07:16.979 --> 00:07:18.270
That's really all it is, right?

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:18.978
There's a number.

00:07:18.978 --> 00:07:21.260
It's a digital world.

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:25.272
If that number gets
compromised, then you

00:07:25.272 --> 00:07:26.480
don't have a secure endpoint.

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:28.040
Doesn't matter what
protocol you use.

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:30.050
Doesn't matter what
algorithm you use.

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:34.220
If I can copy your number,
your device ID, your key,

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:38.457
whatever you want to call
it, then I am you, all right?

00:07:38.457 --> 00:07:40.790
So everything we're going to
think about is going to be,

00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:43.190
how do we take that ultimate
route of trust out of device

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:46.490
key and protect that
in a way that then we

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:48.660
can build upon that to
build a secure system?

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:51.410
And so to do that, you
need a couple of things.

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:53.570
You need to be able to
verify that identity.

00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:55.590
And obviously, if I just
send you my identity

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:58.064
and send over the
network, I'm Kerry.

00:07:58.064 --> 00:07:59.480
Well, everybody
knows that, right?

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:00.650
You can look that up,
so that's not really

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:01.320
a very good identity.

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:03.653
Needs to be something that
can be verified without being

00:08:03.653 --> 00:08:06.235
disclosed, trustable, unique.

00:08:06.235 --> 00:08:08.360
Obviously, it's not good
if there's a lot of Kerrys

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:09.845
out there, and
there probably are.

00:08:09.845 --> 00:08:11.720
So there must be something
different about me

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:13.160
than the others.

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:14.870
We'll talk about that.

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:17.660
This isn't to talk
about cryptography,

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:20.240
but let's just say that
asymmetric cryptography

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:22.002
is a good solution
for this approach.

00:08:22.002 --> 00:08:23.960
Maybe we'll store a
private key as an identity.

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:27.136
We'll use the public key
as a way to validate that.

00:08:27.136 --> 00:08:29.510
I won't talk too much about
how the mechanics of it work,

00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:33.860
but you'll see how that plays
out as we go forward here.

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:37.580
So OK, we got this number, this
digital ID, this private key,

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:38.789
whatever you want to call it.

00:08:38.789 --> 00:08:40.413
I said that's what
everything is about.

00:08:40.413 --> 00:08:41.419
That's what's important.

00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:43.520
Well, how do you get it
in your gadget, right?

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:47.000
How do you put that private key
into your device in such a way

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:49.670
that, well, ideally,
not too many devices

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:53.124
have copies of the same
private key when you mess up.

00:08:53.124 --> 00:08:55.040
You've got a diverse
manufacturer environment.

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:57.500
Maybe you've got subcontractors
on three continents.

00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:59.680
Maybe one guy builds
one part of the system.

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:01.040
Another guy builds another
part of the system.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:03.290
Maybe next year you have a
different subcontinent than

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:04.250
you've got this year.

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:07.970
How do you manage that
environment in such a way

00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:10.910
that the way these
identities go in your device

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:12.590
can really be trusted?

00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:14.090
They're all unique.

00:09:14.090 --> 00:09:15.980
So this is a pretty
complicated problem,

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:19.710
and let me propose a
solution that says,

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:21.560
I don't care where
you manufacture.

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:24.590
Let's move that identity
process, that identity

00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:27.070
assignment process,
out of the manufacturer

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:30.030
sub-cons into the supplier
of the semiconductor.

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:30.760
So you build a--

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:33.920
take a secure element,
secure chip, same thing,

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:36.800
and the manufacturer of the
secure chip-- in this case,

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:39.960
Microchip-- we program
that identity in it.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:42.890
So when it shows up at
the manufacturing sites,

00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:45.565
at your subcontractors, it's
already got that identity.

00:09:45.565 --> 00:09:48.560
And let's go one step
further than that.

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:50.090
Let's build not
only the identity,

00:09:50.090 --> 00:09:52.950
but the relationship between
that identity and something

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:53.450
else.

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:56.330
That relationship might be
manifested in a certificate.

00:09:56.330 --> 00:09:58.550
That's a traditional PKI scheme.

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:01.272
It might be manifested in some
kind of a registration scheme.

00:10:01.272 --> 00:10:03.230
That's what we'll talk
about a little bit later

00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:04.840
in the presentation.

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:08.380
So identity and a relationship
prebuilt into that chip

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:10.490
so that no matter where
you build it in the world,

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:14.600
you get that same
trust of, it's unique.

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:18.320
Nobody was watching when
you put it in the device.

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:19.940
OK, so we got an identity.

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:23.290
We got a way to get
it in your gadget.

00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:24.102
But wait a minute.

00:10:24.102 --> 00:10:26.060
Then the gadget's going
to go out in the field.

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:27.770
There's going to be jillions
of these things, right?

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:29.420
You guys are all big,
successful companies.

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:31.330
You're going to sell millions
and millions and billions

00:10:31.330 --> 00:10:33.530
of devices, and everywhere
around the world,

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:34.890
there's going to be your device.

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:36.931
And because you're so
successful, a lot of people

00:10:36.931 --> 00:10:38.848
are going to want to try
and copy your device.

00:10:38.848 --> 00:10:40.389
And if they want to
copy your device,

00:10:40.389 --> 00:10:42.890
they need to get that digital
ID out of one of them,

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:45.900
and then make 50 copies of that
one or maybe get out of 100.

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:48.729
So you really don't want them
taking the digital ID out

00:10:48.729 --> 00:10:51.020
of your device, and you've
got to protect against that.

00:10:51.020 --> 00:10:54.560
One way you do that, or maybe
the only way to do that,

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:56.270
is to provide some
kind of secure storage

00:10:56.270 --> 00:10:59.540
for that private
key, that secret.

00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:02.484
And let's just say that you
really don't want anybody

00:11:02.484 --> 00:11:03.150
to mess with it.

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:04.080
They can't copy it.

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:04.670
They can't change it.

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:05.390
They can't delete it.

00:11:05.390 --> 00:11:06.848
Any of those things
are really bad.

00:11:06.848 --> 00:11:08.630
Once that happens,
then the device

00:11:08.630 --> 00:11:11.930
really doesn't have a connection
anymore to the network, right?

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:14.820
So that's good, but
it's not enough.

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:16.940
You got to protect
what your device does.

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:18.160
I got the temperature sensor.

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:20.930
I've identified that
it's in room 12.

00:11:20.930 --> 00:11:22.940
But if I can change
the software,

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:25.380
well, yeah, I'm talking
to the thing in room 12,

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:27.380
but no, it's not really
give me the temperature.

00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:28.921
It's giving me a
bogus number, right?

00:11:28.921 --> 00:11:30.740
So you've got to
protect the firmware.

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:32.360
There's lots of ways to do that.

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:35.090
Let's just say one
fairly traditional way

00:11:35.090 --> 00:11:39.320
is to verify a signature over
that code every time the thing

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:40.790
boots, right?

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:43.290
So it's hard to
change the software.

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:45.350
I have to replace the
chip without getting

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:46.430
the thing to reboot.

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:48.567
So let's verify a code
signature at boot.

00:11:48.567 --> 00:11:50.150
A lot of people call
that secure boot.

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:51.400
That seems like a good answer.

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:55.460
We'll connect that a little
bit later to hardware security.

00:11:55.460 --> 00:11:58.510
Now, Zoltan made a mention
about key rotation.

00:11:58.510 --> 00:12:00.272
And you might say to
me, whoa, whoa, whoa,

00:12:00.272 --> 00:12:01.730
you just said you're
going to store

00:12:01.730 --> 00:12:03.020
this thing really super-secure.

00:12:03.020 --> 00:12:04.220
Nobody's going to
be able to touch it.

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:05.345
Why do I need to rotate it?

00:12:05.345 --> 00:12:08.310
Hey, nobody can mess with it.

00:12:08.310 --> 00:12:12.180
Well, there could be
rules, regulations,

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:13.370
policies that demand it.

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:14.245
That's OK, all right?

00:12:14.245 --> 00:12:15.911
We've all got rules
we've got to follow.

00:12:15.911 --> 00:12:17.730
Sometimes they don't
make any sense.

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:18.650
That's just life.

00:12:18.650 --> 00:12:20.480
So there could be policies.

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:22.130
There could be limits.

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:23.870
Every algorithm has
a limit on the number

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:28.160
of times it can be used before
the key is no longer considered

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:29.760
to be valid.

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:30.590
So that's OK.

00:12:30.590 --> 00:12:32.909
I don't mind that.

00:12:32.909 --> 00:12:34.700
So you want to be able
to do this rotation,

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:37.550
but at the same
time, you can't think

00:12:37.550 --> 00:12:41.260
that rotation is a substitute
for security, right?

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:44.290
If I say, well, I don't need
to secure the key [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:47.940
because next week, I'm going to
have a new key, so who cares?

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:48.820
It's only one week.

00:12:48.820 --> 00:12:50.620
That isn't going to work, right?

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:52.328
Because the bad guys
are going to find it

00:12:52.328 --> 00:12:54.304
not next week, not next day.

00:12:54.304 --> 00:12:56.470
They're going find it three
seconds after you rotate

00:12:56.470 --> 00:12:59.050
that key, and if
you don't have a way

00:12:59.050 --> 00:13:01.810
to protect that key for
that week, that year,

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:05.210
that month, that 10
million transactions,

00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:06.940
then you still
don't have security.

00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:09.400
So key rotation is
important-- got to do it--

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:12.370
but it's no substitute for
an environment in which you

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:14.620
didn't have to do it.

00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:15.580
So think about that.

00:13:15.580 --> 00:13:16.913
There's lots of ways to do that.

00:13:16.913 --> 00:13:18.940
I'm not going to talk
about it too much.

00:13:18.940 --> 00:13:22.064
PKI or asymmetric cryptography
gives you a couple of ways.

00:13:22.064 --> 00:13:23.980
You can either generate
new keys and sign them

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:25.600
with a trusted key.

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:27.740
You can use a
derivation mechanism.

00:13:27.740 --> 00:13:30.055
There are lots out
there that are good.

00:13:30.055 --> 00:13:31.930
You could store multiple
keys in your device.

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:33.550
You could ship it with 10
keys, and we could say,

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:35.508
I'm going to use the keys
[? that ?] [? ran, ?]

00:13:35.508 --> 00:13:37.330
or it's got a
10-year life cycle.

00:13:37.330 --> 00:13:38.770
I'm going to use the first
key for the first year

00:13:38.770 --> 00:13:40.160
and the second key
for the second year,

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:41.243
however you want to do it.

00:13:41.243 --> 00:13:42.230
That's fine.

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:43.600
It's all good.

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:45.905
And the cloud is going to
manage those policies, right?

00:13:45.905 --> 00:13:47.905
So the cloud understands
the trust relationship.

00:13:47.905 --> 00:13:50.887
The cloud understands the
architecture of the device,

00:13:50.887 --> 00:13:53.470
and the cloud can say, OK, hey,
I don't like that key anymore.

00:13:53.470 --> 00:13:55.120
Let's go on to the
next key, right?

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:58.450
So that's the good thing about
having the cloud relationship.

00:13:58.450 --> 00:14:01.400
No longer does the device
have to be able to say, hey,

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:03.250
I don't like this key anymore.

00:14:03.250 --> 00:14:06.420
Now the cloud can manage that.

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:11.010
So Zoltan touched on this.

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:12.030
You build your system.

00:14:12.030 --> 00:14:13.680
You got all kinds of software.

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:15.409
You love all your
software solutions.

00:14:15.409 --> 00:14:16.200
You got this stack.

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:17.190
You got that stack.

00:14:17.190 --> 00:14:19.194
Maybe you downloaded
some software.

00:14:19.194 --> 00:14:20.610
But at the end of
the day, there's

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:24.487
that thing that I started
with, that digital ID, right?

00:14:24.487 --> 00:14:25.320
It's got to be used.

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:29.130
You got to put it as input to
some complicated algorithm,

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:32.480
and that means the software
has got to touch that.

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:34.070
So there's a piece
of software that

00:14:34.070 --> 00:14:38.520
has access to that digital
ID, that private key.

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:41.322
So if one piece of
software can touch it,

00:14:41.322 --> 00:14:43.530
how are you going to prevent
other pieces of software

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:44.238
from touching it?

00:14:44.238 --> 00:14:45.010
Oh, you got an OS.

00:14:45.010 --> 00:14:46.170
You got kernel code.

00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:47.820
Yeah, sure.

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:51.240
We all know all kinds of
exploits which allow things

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:55.360
that you wish wouldn't touch
stuff to actually touch them.

00:14:55.360 --> 00:14:58.470
So especially in
the IoT world, where

00:14:58.470 --> 00:14:59.970
you've got these
complicated things,

00:14:59.970 --> 00:15:02.666
you got all these stacks doing
all these different things,

00:15:02.666 --> 00:15:04.290
but you're a temperature
sensor, right?

00:15:04.290 --> 00:15:05.706
What's the temperature
in room 12?

00:15:05.706 --> 00:15:08.310
But it's really complicated
to do that, right?

00:15:08.310 --> 00:15:10.470
So what we're going
to propose here

00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:13.554
is I'm going to isolate
those private keys from all

00:15:13.554 --> 00:15:14.220
of the software.

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:17.620
I'm going to say no software
can touch the private keys.

00:15:17.620 --> 00:15:19.420
I don't care what
software you have.

00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:22.780
Good, bad, big,
small, purple, green--

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:24.250
doesn't touch the private key.

00:15:24.250 --> 00:15:28.445
And by doing that, it
leaves a lot of questions,

00:15:28.445 --> 00:15:30.070
a lot of problems
you have to validate.

00:15:30.070 --> 00:15:32.490
It takes those away, right?

00:15:32.490 --> 00:15:35.770
And by the way, it's not
that we don't like users.

00:15:35.770 --> 00:15:37.320
It's not that we
don't trust them.

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:39.360
It's that they make
poor choices, right?

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:41.835
OK, how many of us
have ever logged on

00:15:41.835 --> 00:15:43.710
and created a password
that may not have been

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:44.910
an optimal password, right?

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:45.240
Once?

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:45.810
Twice?

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:46.410
It happens, right?

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:47.440
So you can't really trust users.

00:15:47.440 --> 00:15:48.898
It doesn't mean
they're bad people.

00:15:48.898 --> 00:15:50.580
It doesn't mean
they're untrustworthy.

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:53.490
It just means that they
make choices that may not

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:55.790
be consistent with security.

00:15:55.790 --> 00:15:57.930
So you're not going to
let a user see it, not

00:15:57.930 --> 00:15:59.640
let its software see it.

00:15:59.640 --> 00:16:01.419
And when we talk
about hardware, you

00:16:01.419 --> 00:16:03.210
might think, oh, I've
got a microprocessor.

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:04.640
That's a piece of
hardware, right?

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:06.330
No, not really.

00:16:06.330 --> 00:16:09.420
Microprocessors are
typically not designed

00:16:09.420 --> 00:16:12.330
to protect keys in a way that
an aggressive attack might

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:14.163
get them, and I'll show
you in a minute what

00:16:14.163 --> 00:16:16.150
an aggressive attack means.

00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:19.740
And if you think about it, if
you connect all these dots,

00:16:19.740 --> 00:16:22.980
now, that means that it's also
protected in the manufacturing

00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:26.307
phase, because even the
manufacturer doesn't have a way

00:16:26.307 --> 00:16:27.140
to attack it, right?

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:29.750
So you've got some sub-con
soldering chips on the board.

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:31.070
Doesn't matter.

00:16:31.070 --> 00:16:33.860
He could be just as
untrusted as some guy

00:16:33.860 --> 00:16:37.130
out in the field who hacked the
sensor off the wall of room 12,

00:16:37.130 --> 00:16:37.910
right?

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:42.400
So totally protected during
the manufacturing phase.

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:43.720
So OK, that's nice.

00:16:43.720 --> 00:16:45.520
These are all nice concepts.

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:47.620
Now, how do we build
that into something

00:16:47.620 --> 00:16:50.500
that's useful that we
can ship to customers

00:16:50.500 --> 00:16:52.840
that connect to the cloud
and do a useful thing?

00:16:52.840 --> 00:16:55.080
It's got to have value, right?

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:57.660
So first thing,
use a secure chip.

00:16:57.660 --> 00:17:01.110
So I'm going to talk a little
bit here about the ATECC608A.

00:17:04.609 --> 00:17:07.550
So I won't say that
too much, you know?

00:17:07.550 --> 00:17:09.109
Think of it as a
secure element, OK?

00:17:09.109 --> 00:17:10.490
It's a secure chip.

00:17:10.490 --> 00:17:12.854
Think of it as an
electronic safe.

00:17:12.854 --> 00:17:13.770
What's it going to do?

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:14.541
It's going to do--

00:17:14.541 --> 00:17:16.790
of course, it's going to
store that digital ID, right?

00:17:16.790 --> 00:17:17.810
It's got some secure
[? W probably. ?]

00:17:17.810 --> 00:17:19.430
It's going to put
the key in there.

00:17:19.430 --> 00:17:20.010
It's just a key, right?

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:20.859
It's just a number.

00:17:20.859 --> 00:17:22.550
Going to store that
there, and it's

00:17:22.550 --> 00:17:27.020
going to include enough
crypto engines on it

00:17:27.020 --> 00:17:28.809
so it can act on that
key without having

00:17:28.809 --> 00:17:29.600
to divulge the key.

00:17:29.600 --> 00:17:31.850
It wouldn't be very interesting
if I had to send the key out,

00:17:31.850 --> 00:17:32.350
right?

00:17:32.350 --> 00:17:34.220
The key never, ever
leaves the chip.

00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:35.720
You want to do an
authorization, you

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:37.910
want to do some
kind of validation,

00:17:37.910 --> 00:17:42.260
you say, OK, hey, prove
that you have that secret

00:17:42.260 --> 00:17:44.830
without divulging the secret.

00:17:44.830 --> 00:17:48.130
And so all of that's done
inside this boundary.

00:17:48.130 --> 00:17:49.690
So there's a hardware boundary.

00:17:49.690 --> 00:17:51.880
You try and get through the
boundary as an attacker,

00:17:51.880 --> 00:17:53.540
and you can't, all right?

00:17:53.540 --> 00:17:54.600
So it's all encapsulated.

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:56.480
There's no software inside.

00:17:56.480 --> 00:17:58.784
It's not something
where you write the code

00:17:58.784 --> 00:18:00.450
and maybe there's
some bugs in the code.

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:03.970
It's all a turnkey solution.

00:18:03.970 --> 00:18:06.940
And I'll just point out
the red box over there.

00:18:06.940 --> 00:18:08.690
I like to point this
out because everybody

00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:10.069
thinks, well, random numbers.

00:18:10.069 --> 00:18:10.610
I don't know.

00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:11.651
Just get some keystrokes.

00:18:11.651 --> 00:18:13.100
It doesn't matter, right?

00:18:13.100 --> 00:18:16.610
Well, actually, every single
cryptographic protocol

00:18:16.610 --> 00:18:22.080
out there, one way or the
other, requires random numbers,

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:25.170
and random numbers are
really hard to generate.

00:18:25.170 --> 00:18:27.960
So my favorite definition
of a random number--

00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:30.600
one that's never been
generated before and will never

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:33.030
be generated again, right?

00:18:33.030 --> 00:18:34.230
That's a random number.

00:18:34.230 --> 00:18:35.855
It's maybe not a
technical description,

00:18:35.855 --> 00:18:38.040
but it's not a bad
functional description,

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:40.750
and it's really hard to do that
in a way that you can trust,

00:18:40.750 --> 00:18:41.250
right?

00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:44.730
Everybody's got their
multi-gigahertz, multi-core

00:18:44.730 --> 00:18:45.240
processor.

00:18:45.240 --> 00:18:48.617
Think how many times it
can try different numbers.

00:18:48.617 --> 00:18:50.700
So if you want a number
that's different this time

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:53.267
than last time, it had
better be really good,

00:18:53.267 --> 00:18:54.600
and it's really hard to do that.

00:18:54.600 --> 00:18:57.859
So I'm going to put all
those things inside this box.

00:18:57.859 --> 00:19:00.150
And that means you hae a
simple interface to it, right?

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:04.650
I-squared-C, one wire,
so easy to talk to,

00:19:04.650 --> 00:19:06.460
but hard to get inside.

00:19:06.460 --> 00:19:11.296
So that's the basic concept
of a secure element.

00:19:11.296 --> 00:19:11.920
OK, I got this.

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:12.840
What good is it?

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:13.830
Because it's all
by itself, right?

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:15.705
That's not the temperature
sensor in room 12.

00:19:15.705 --> 00:19:18.090
That's just a little box
with a device ID in it.

00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:19.350
How are you going to use it?

00:19:19.350 --> 00:19:20.790
Well, you're going to
connect it to whatever

00:19:20.790 --> 00:19:21.970
you've got on your system.

00:19:21.970 --> 00:19:24.490
So you've got some kind
of processing element.

00:19:24.490 --> 00:19:26.700
Maybe it's the Edge TPU
that just got announced.

00:19:26.700 --> 00:19:31.080
Maybe it's an MCU,
an MPU, and that guy

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:32.190
is going to talk to it.

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:34.530
Now, the slide here
shows something

00:19:34.530 --> 00:19:37.380
like where the MCU passes
a challenge to the chip.

00:19:37.380 --> 00:19:39.540
The chip responds
with a response,

00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:42.320
and the MCU has some
way of verifying that.

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:43.790
That's a fine answer.

00:19:43.790 --> 00:19:45.560
Well, we're actually
going to show later

00:19:45.560 --> 00:19:47.960
in the presentation in the
demo is the cloud sending

00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:52.160
a challenge down through the
microprocessor to the chip.

00:19:52.160 --> 00:19:55.067
The chip responds,
sends that response back

00:19:55.067 --> 00:19:56.900
through the microprocessor,
up to the cloud,

00:19:56.900 --> 00:19:58.566
and then then cloud
decides whether it's

00:19:58.566 --> 00:20:00.920
going to accept this
transaction, this relationship

00:20:00.920 --> 00:20:02.430
or not, right?

00:20:02.430 --> 00:20:08.630
So I said at the beginning
that asymmetric cryptography

00:20:08.630 --> 00:20:12.860
and private keys make a nice
way to think about a device ID.

00:20:12.860 --> 00:20:13.550
I stand by that.

00:20:13.550 --> 00:20:18.020
It's beautiful, but there
are downsides to PKI,

00:20:18.020 --> 00:20:21.920
and some people like symmetric
or shared identification

00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:23.400
as well.

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:24.800
I'm totally cool with that.

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:25.710
That's a good idea.

00:20:25.710 --> 00:20:27.290
There's a time and
a place for that.

00:20:27.290 --> 00:20:29.780
So that challenge
and response-- it

00:20:29.780 --> 00:20:34.180
could be a challenge in a
signature if it's asymmetric.

00:20:34.180 --> 00:20:36.620
It could be a challenge in
a Mac if it's symmetric.

00:20:36.620 --> 00:20:38.120
Those are perfectly
valid things,

00:20:38.120 --> 00:20:41.180
and I would never say that one
is more secure than the other.

00:20:41.180 --> 00:20:43.010
One may be more
appropriate than the other,

00:20:43.010 --> 00:20:44.780
but they're both secure
ways to accomplish

00:20:44.780 --> 00:20:50.250
this problem of verifiable
identification, right?

00:20:50.250 --> 00:20:52.970
And then, let me just make a
small point for those of you

00:20:52.970 --> 00:20:57.170
out there who have designed
PC boards and the like.

00:20:57.170 --> 00:20:58.760
This is a tiny,
little thing, right?

00:20:58.760 --> 00:21:02.690
It's not your video
processor with 300 I/Os.

00:21:02.690 --> 00:21:06.380
It's a tiny, little box with
a couple of wires for I/O.

00:21:06.380 --> 00:21:09.840
And when Lucio does
his demonstration,

00:21:09.840 --> 00:21:12.410
you'll see it's probably the
smallest chip on the board,

00:21:12.410 --> 00:21:13.850
even though it has the
most security of all

00:21:13.850 --> 00:21:14.891
those chips on the board.

00:21:14.891 --> 00:21:17.900
So OK, we're going to think
about this secure element now.

00:21:17.900 --> 00:21:21.080
It fits whatever your
endpoint looks like.

00:21:21.080 --> 00:21:26.180
I don't care whether it's
got a 32-bit, 64-bit, 8-core

00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:30.200
processor, or it's an 8-bit,
single-threaded machine

00:21:30.200 --> 00:21:31.820
with K bytes of memory.

00:21:31.820 --> 00:21:32.450
Doesn't matter.

00:21:32.450 --> 00:21:34.104
And everything in between--

00:21:34.104 --> 00:21:36.020
you can put this on that
board, and now you've

00:21:36.020 --> 00:21:38.085
got a route of
trust that you can

00:21:38.085 --> 00:21:40.040
trust separate from
all your software,

00:21:40.040 --> 00:21:44.620
from all the other
pieces of your system.

00:21:44.620 --> 00:21:49.340
So key protection-- great.

00:21:49.340 --> 00:21:49.960
I'm up here.

00:21:49.960 --> 00:21:50.996
I'm selling this thing.

00:21:50.996 --> 00:21:52.870
I say, hey, it's really
strong with the keys.

00:21:52.870 --> 00:21:54.130
Trust me, right?

00:21:54.130 --> 00:21:56.350
Well, maybe you don't.

00:21:56.350 --> 00:21:58.480
So how is it that people
get these keys out, right?

00:21:58.480 --> 00:22:01.680
I mean, lots of
software attacks--

00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:05.860
Zoltan's slide showed a bunch
of different software attacks.

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:09.529
But you know, if you're
making a billion gadgets,

00:22:09.529 --> 00:22:11.320
you've got a lot--
somebody else would like

00:22:11.320 --> 00:22:12.620
to steal your market, right?

00:22:12.620 --> 00:22:15.850
10% or 1% of your billion
gadget market is a big number.

00:22:15.850 --> 00:22:18.340
I'd really like to
have that as a bad guy.

00:22:18.340 --> 00:22:21.970
And so I'm not going to stop at
some published attack, right?

00:22:21.970 --> 00:22:24.310
I'm going to do all sorts
of crazy things, like--

00:22:24.310 --> 00:22:27.700
this particular crazy thing I
got up there is a micro-prober.

00:22:27.700 --> 00:22:29.377
You could scrape the
top off the chip.

00:22:29.377 --> 00:22:31.210
You could put some
needles down on the chip.

00:22:31.210 --> 00:22:33.126
And trust me, this is
not a complicated thing.

00:22:33.126 --> 00:22:34.965
You can buy
micro-probers on eBay.

00:22:34.965 --> 00:22:36.340
You can use them
in your kitchen.

00:22:36.340 --> 00:22:39.671
It doesn't really require
anything too complicated.

00:22:39.671 --> 00:22:41.170
Really, you just
kind of probe down.

00:22:41.170 --> 00:22:43.461
You know that JTAG lock that
you got on your processor?

00:22:43.461 --> 00:22:46.000
You hit that bit, and
no more JTAG, right?

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:49.090
Unless I put a micro-probe
down on that JTAG wire

00:22:49.090 --> 00:22:51.850
and I pull it high and say,
oh, now I do have JTAG,

00:22:51.850 --> 00:22:55.540
and I reuse JTAG to
read the key out, right?

00:22:55.540 --> 00:22:56.920
So if you get a secure element--

00:22:56.920 --> 00:22:59.230
there's a picture of it,
a piece and a blow-up

00:22:59.230 --> 00:23:00.415
of a piece of that.

00:23:00.415 --> 00:23:02.290
Secure elements typically
will have a shield.

00:23:02.290 --> 00:23:06.070
So that serpentine thing on the
side covers the entire chip.

00:23:06.070 --> 00:23:07.862
There's nothing to look
at, and the signals

00:23:07.862 --> 00:23:09.778
that are going through
that have nothing to do

00:23:09.778 --> 00:23:11.080
with cryptographic or identity.

00:23:11.080 --> 00:23:13.480
They're just random
signals that we check,

00:23:13.480 --> 00:23:15.850
and if you short
them out or cut them,

00:23:15.850 --> 00:23:19.245
then the chip stops
working and it's dead.

00:23:19.245 --> 00:23:21.420
Look, I'm only showing
this particular attack

00:23:21.420 --> 00:23:24.860
because it really
looks good on a slide.

00:23:24.860 --> 00:23:27.910
Well, there's pictures,
shields, stuff like that.

00:23:27.910 --> 00:23:30.070
There are way better
attacks than this one.

00:23:30.070 --> 00:23:31.570
This one's
complicated, you know?

00:23:31.570 --> 00:23:33.070
Probably, people
are going to notice

00:23:33.070 --> 00:23:36.730
if you take your thing apart
and strip the silicon off.

00:23:36.730 --> 00:23:43.129
My favorite non-visible
attack is some kind

00:23:43.129 --> 00:23:44.170
of a side channel attack.

00:23:44.170 --> 00:23:47.270
You put a resistor in
series with the battery.

00:23:47.270 --> 00:23:50.290
You do single trace or
maybe multiple traces

00:23:50.290 --> 00:23:53.800
of the power signature during
a cryptographic operation.

00:23:53.800 --> 00:23:57.610
You take that trace away and you
put the battery cover back on.

00:23:57.610 --> 00:23:59.380
Hang the thing back on the wall.

00:23:59.380 --> 00:24:02.660
And now, at your leisure,
you analyze that trace.

00:24:02.660 --> 00:24:05.500
And using that analysis,
you can find the key.

00:24:05.500 --> 00:24:08.310
And so if you think that
anybody's microprocessor won't

00:24:08.310 --> 00:24:11.774
leak data when it's
executing code--

00:24:11.774 --> 00:24:13.440
this isn't the right
slide presentation,

00:24:13.440 --> 00:24:16.000
but I got some really good
ones to show you how it's done.

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:17.710
It's actually not that hard.

00:24:17.710 --> 00:24:20.820
So there are hundreds
of different attacks,

00:24:20.820 --> 00:24:22.897
and you have to defend
against every one, right?

00:24:22.897 --> 00:24:24.480
Because of course
the attacker is only

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:26.430
going to do the weakest link.

00:24:26.430 --> 00:24:28.440
Whatever one thing you
didn't defend against,

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:29.490
that's what the
attacker is going to do,

00:24:29.490 --> 00:24:32.400
and it won't matter that you
defended against the other 99.

00:24:32.400 --> 00:24:34.440
So you need to have
a way to protect

00:24:34.440 --> 00:24:38.250
these secrets that defends
against everything, right?

00:24:38.250 --> 00:24:40.060
Attacks you've never heard of--

00:24:40.060 --> 00:24:42.440
and so that's probably not
going to happen by default.

00:24:42.440 --> 00:24:45.360
It's going to happen
because somebody

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:50.080
has spent careers and teams
working on just that problem.

00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:52.080
And so that's what a
secure element does, right?

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:54.870
The whole point of using
a secure element like this

00:24:54.870 --> 00:24:57.904
is that that's all we
do every day is think

00:24:57.904 --> 00:25:00.570
about how the bad guys are going
to try and get the secrets out.

00:25:00.570 --> 00:25:03.032
We don't know anything about
temperature sensors, really.

00:25:03.032 --> 00:25:05.490
All we know about is that bad
guys are going to try and get

00:25:05.490 --> 00:25:06.615
the secret out of our chip.

00:25:06.615 --> 00:25:09.710
We don't want to
let that happen.

00:25:09.710 --> 00:25:15.110
OK, so let's build that
up now into an IoT thing.

00:25:15.110 --> 00:25:19.250
So this is sort of how
IoT devices look, right?

00:25:19.250 --> 00:25:20.980
You've got some
application, your IP,

00:25:20.980 --> 00:25:23.640
the thing that makes
your device special.

00:25:23.640 --> 00:25:26.590
You got some sort of
cloud connectivity stack,

00:25:26.590 --> 00:25:29.346
ideally connectivity
to Google Cloud, right?

00:25:29.346 --> 00:25:31.720
Because everybody wants the
cloud, and without the cloud,

00:25:31.720 --> 00:25:32.770
how are you going to
get to your device?

00:25:32.770 --> 00:25:33.970
What good is it?

00:25:33.970 --> 00:25:35.810
You've got some sort
of operating system.

00:25:35.810 --> 00:25:38.680
Maybe it's a real-time
operating system, maybe not.

00:25:38.680 --> 00:25:40.960
You've got some big
processing element

00:25:40.960 --> 00:25:42.550
that's running
your communication

00:25:42.550 --> 00:25:46.420
stack and your security
stack, in this case looking

00:25:46.420 --> 00:25:49.914
at something like
TCP/IP and TLS.

00:25:49.914 --> 00:25:50.830
They're common things.

00:25:50.830 --> 00:25:52.397
There are other ways to do it.

00:25:52.397 --> 00:25:54.730
And then you've got some
radios, some wireless hardware,

00:25:54.730 --> 00:25:56.210
and some sensors
to do your thing.

00:25:56.210 --> 00:25:57.626
That's kind of how
things-- that's

00:25:57.626 --> 00:25:59.590
how an IoT device looks, right?

00:25:59.590 --> 00:26:00.830
That's how it's done.

00:26:00.830 --> 00:26:04.490
But let's think about
it for a minute.

00:26:04.490 --> 00:26:08.540
In the world,
there's a big variety

00:26:08.540 --> 00:26:13.220
of architectures and cost
points and power points

00:26:13.220 --> 00:26:15.300
for embedded systems.

00:26:15.300 --> 00:26:17.640
You've got-- everybody
is holding up

00:26:17.640 --> 00:26:18.890
the red one in the top, right?

00:26:18.890 --> 00:26:20.598
You've got some big,
multi-core processor

00:26:20.598 --> 00:26:23.101
with gigabytes and fancy
OSes, and that's great.

00:26:23.101 --> 00:26:24.600
There's a place and
a time for that.

00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:27.060
I wouldn't want anything
less in my pocket.

00:26:27.060 --> 00:26:29.520
But that might not be the right
answer for the temperature

00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:31.740
sensor in room 23.

00:26:31.740 --> 00:26:34.140
Maybe you want something
that's a little bit lower cost.

00:26:34.140 --> 00:26:35.556
So you could think
about something

00:26:35.556 --> 00:26:38.070
in the middle, some kind
of RTOS sort of thing,

00:26:38.070 --> 00:26:41.520
maybe a little bit more
restricted operating

00:26:41.520 --> 00:26:44.110
system, maybe a real-time
operating system.

00:26:44.110 --> 00:26:45.224
That's a good thing.

00:26:45.224 --> 00:26:46.890
Millions of those
units are sold a year,

00:26:46.890 --> 00:26:52.290
but you know, you really like
Zoltan's slide about that 8.4

00:26:52.290 --> 00:26:54.390
billion and that curve.

00:26:54.390 --> 00:26:56.250
You want to be on the
billions of sides.

00:26:56.250 --> 00:26:58.320
You want to sell
billions of devices.

00:26:58.320 --> 00:27:00.150
If you're going to do
that, and if you're

00:27:00.150 --> 00:27:03.252
going to consume that data, if
you're going to think about,

00:27:03.252 --> 00:27:05.460
how do I manage the heating
and cooling in a building

00:27:05.460 --> 00:27:07.170
with 1,000 rooms?

00:27:07.170 --> 00:27:10.430
I need 1,000 temperature
sensors, right?

00:27:10.430 --> 00:27:16.114
If I want to manage some kind
of a factory with 1,000 valves,

00:27:16.114 --> 00:27:17.780
I need to know each
one of those valves.

00:27:17.780 --> 00:27:19.700
I need to control them, right?

00:27:19.700 --> 00:27:22.856
So now we want to think
about these billions of units

00:27:22.856 --> 00:27:24.980
where you want to power
them with 8-bit processors.

00:27:24.980 --> 00:27:29.660
And so AVR and PIC
microprocessors are probably

00:27:29.660 --> 00:27:31.590
the kind of processors
you're going to use.

00:27:31.590 --> 00:27:33.860
They're the number one
and number two processors

00:27:33.860 --> 00:27:34.860
in the market.

00:27:34.860 --> 00:27:37.000
But these are very
resource-constrained things.

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:38.960
Don't think gigabytes of memory.

00:27:38.960 --> 00:27:40.770
Think Kbytes of memory.

00:27:40.770 --> 00:27:44.918
And don't think how many--

00:27:44.918 --> 00:27:47.210
how big is the power
supply going to be?

00:27:47.210 --> 00:27:50.570
Think, can I make that
battery last for five years

00:27:50.570 --> 00:27:52.080
without replacing it?

00:27:52.080 --> 00:27:55.160
All right, so we're talking
about a totally different

00:27:55.160 --> 00:27:59.300
environment here, and this
is the thing that drives

00:27:59.300 --> 00:28:01.450
that 8.4 billion number, right?

00:28:01.450 --> 00:28:04.320
It's this constrained
environment.

00:28:04.320 --> 00:28:09.560
So how do I simplify the
development of an IoT device

00:28:09.560 --> 00:28:11.360
so that I can build
those small devices

00:28:11.360 --> 00:28:13.010
and I can get them to
market in a timely manner?

00:28:13.010 --> 00:28:14.720
Because the other guy is trying
to build the same thing, right?

00:28:14.720 --> 00:28:18.020
If I don't get there sooner,
he's going to take my market.

00:28:18.020 --> 00:28:21.004
So let's redraw that picture
a little bit differently.

00:28:21.004 --> 00:28:22.670
You still got your
application software.

00:28:22.670 --> 00:28:25.762
Whatever makes you special,
you still got to do that.

00:28:25.762 --> 00:28:27.720
You got some kind of
cloud connectivity stacks,

00:28:27.720 --> 00:28:30.740
but now let's reduce that
to the component pieces

00:28:30.740 --> 00:28:33.060
necessary to talk to a stack.

00:28:33.060 --> 00:28:36.091
Here's the component
pieces for Google Cloud.

00:28:36.091 --> 00:28:38.090
And beyond that, now we're
going to really start

00:28:38.090 --> 00:28:38.630
to change things.

00:28:38.630 --> 00:28:41.213
We're going to say, OK, I want
a development environment where

00:28:41.213 --> 00:28:43.430
I can automatically
generate the drivers I need,

00:28:43.430 --> 00:28:45.638
because really, I only care
about the stuff in yellow

00:28:45.638 --> 00:28:47.690
or orange or whatever
that color is up there.

00:28:47.690 --> 00:28:49.550
I only care about what's my IP.

00:28:49.550 --> 00:28:51.500
I want to push the
button, one click,

00:28:51.500 --> 00:28:53.720
and get all that
other stuff done.

00:28:53.720 --> 00:28:56.340
And at the lowest level, I
want to split the security

00:28:56.340 --> 00:28:59.150
and communication stacks
away from my processor.

00:28:59.150 --> 00:29:02.690
I want to focus on writing the
software in my process, my MCU,

00:29:02.690 --> 00:29:04.620
that does what's
important to me--

00:29:04.620 --> 00:29:06.110
processes that
temperature, right?

00:29:06.110 --> 00:29:09.820
It was the number-- not that
complicated, in a sense.

00:29:09.820 --> 00:29:11.570
And I'm going to leave
the security stack.

00:29:11.570 --> 00:29:12.840
I'm going to put it
in a secure element.

00:29:12.840 --> 00:29:14.090
I'm going to take the
connectivity stack,

00:29:14.090 --> 00:29:16.310
I'm going to go put it
in a wireless element.

00:29:16.310 --> 00:29:20.610
And now I'm going to build a
much simpler and more secure

00:29:20.610 --> 00:29:21.739
IoT element.

00:29:21.739 --> 00:29:23.780
And I'm going to get to
market faster because I'm

00:29:23.780 --> 00:29:26.390
going to do it with
much less software

00:29:26.390 --> 00:29:29.090
and in a development environment
where I can push a button

00:29:29.090 --> 00:29:30.810
and get it done, right?

00:29:30.810 --> 00:29:33.026
And that's what we're all about.

00:29:33.026 --> 00:29:36.674
So just so you don't
think this is vaporware.

00:29:36.674 --> 00:29:37.840
I mean, it's a good concept.

00:29:37.840 --> 00:29:40.040
But if we can't
reduce it to practice,

00:29:40.040 --> 00:29:41.410
then that's not so interesting.

00:29:41.410 --> 00:29:43.900
So Lucio's going to show a
demonstration later where

00:29:43.900 --> 00:29:46.780
we connect these four things.

00:29:46.780 --> 00:29:49.450
The demonstration happens
to use this particular AVR

00:29:49.450 --> 00:29:53.740
microcontroller, but this
concept, this architecture,

00:29:53.740 --> 00:29:56.890
this software works with
any one of the thousands

00:29:56.890 --> 00:30:00.880
of microcontrollers that
we and other people sell.

00:30:00.880 --> 00:30:05.910
What we offer is a one click
software development approach.

00:30:05.910 --> 00:30:08.890
So doesn't matter which
of the processors you use.

00:30:08.890 --> 00:30:10.720
You push that button,
you get the software

00:30:10.720 --> 00:30:13.210
stack necessary to do it.

00:30:13.210 --> 00:30:15.940
The secure element that
we've been talking about here

00:30:15.940 --> 00:30:18.190
implements all the algorithms,
whether it's symmetric,

00:30:18.190 --> 00:30:22.540
whether it's asymmetric
to do that cryptography,

00:30:22.540 --> 00:30:25.817
secure that key, and to
isolate your software stack

00:30:25.817 --> 00:30:26.900
from all the cryptography.

00:30:26.900 --> 00:30:29.020
So for instance, I
talked about secure boot,

00:30:29.020 --> 00:30:30.810
validating your code image.

00:30:30.810 --> 00:30:33.402
Well, that secure element
can validate the signature.

00:30:33.402 --> 00:30:35.110
So instead of you have
thousands of bytes

00:30:35.110 --> 00:30:37.990
and maybe minutes of
code execution time,

00:30:37.990 --> 00:30:40.600
you push that hard stuff
off to the cryptoprocessor,

00:30:40.600 --> 00:30:43.260
it does that software stack.

00:30:43.260 --> 00:30:44.820
Same for Wi-Fi.

00:30:44.820 --> 00:30:49.200
This particular picture
shows a Wi-Fi chip.

00:30:49.200 --> 00:30:51.390
We've got solutions with
other networks as well.

00:30:51.390 --> 00:30:53.940
The one we're showing here,
despite the congested Wi-Fi

00:30:53.940 --> 00:30:58.470
environment, is the WINC1510.

00:30:58.470 --> 00:31:00.402
It's got the TLS
and TCP/IP stacks

00:31:00.402 --> 00:31:01.860
built in so you
don't have to think

00:31:01.860 --> 00:31:03.270
about that in your application.

00:31:03.270 --> 00:31:07.020
Think about it as, you send
some bytes, get some bytes.

00:31:07.020 --> 00:31:10.600
And it all would be useless if
there was nobody to talk to.

00:31:10.600 --> 00:31:13.150
So it's all set
up in the same way

00:31:13.150 --> 00:31:15.610
to talk to Google IoT Cloud.

00:31:15.610 --> 00:31:19.030
And so now you've got
this easy to design

00:31:19.030 --> 00:31:23.710
solution with an easy to
use cloud environment,

00:31:23.710 --> 00:31:26.040
and that's exactly what your
customers are looking for.

00:31:26.040 --> 00:31:28.000
And it's highly secure.

00:31:28.000 --> 00:31:29.700
So when you go
down and attack it,

00:31:29.700 --> 00:31:33.400
you're not going to
get that private key.

00:31:33.400 --> 00:31:34.820
So let me pass off to Zoltan.

00:31:39.204 --> 00:31:40.370
ZOLTAN ARATO: Thanks, Kerry.

00:31:40.370 --> 00:31:43.700
So just to reiterate,
there are two sides

00:31:43.700 --> 00:31:46.700
of providing highly secure
end to end deployment,

00:31:46.700 --> 00:31:48.830
a secure chip, and the cloud.

00:31:48.830 --> 00:31:52.990
So let's talk a little bit
about the cloud side of things.

00:31:52.990 --> 00:31:56.220
So three key challenges
we kept in mind

00:31:56.220 --> 00:32:02.250
while building an IoT solution,
and while developing Cloud IoT.

00:32:02.250 --> 00:32:03.720
Secure device connection.

00:32:03.720 --> 00:32:06.150
Probably needless to
talk more about this.

00:32:06.150 --> 00:32:08.100
That's the topic of
the presentation.

00:32:08.100 --> 00:32:11.700
Scaling on demand, meaning
you start with a prototype,

00:32:11.700 --> 00:32:14.400
you deploy 10, 20, 100 devices.

00:32:14.400 --> 00:32:16.500
And when you feel
like it's time,

00:32:16.500 --> 00:32:19.180
you can easily go to a million.

00:32:19.180 --> 00:32:20.450
And actionable insights.

00:32:20.450 --> 00:32:24.720
So these devices
generate lots of data,

00:32:24.720 --> 00:32:27.330
and you have to look
behind that data.

00:32:27.330 --> 00:32:28.650
What's behind that data?

00:32:28.650 --> 00:32:32.430
What kind of analytics you
can get out of that data,

00:32:32.430 --> 00:32:34.300
what you read out of that data.

00:32:34.300 --> 00:32:39.120
And so, these are the principles
we kept in mind while designing

00:32:39.120 --> 00:32:41.310
Google Cloud IoT.

00:32:41.310 --> 00:32:44.940
So this is just an
overview of Cloud IoT Core

00:32:44.940 --> 00:32:48.000
and the whole
ecosystem around it.

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:52.830
Cloud IoT Core is our solution
for connecting devices,

00:32:52.830 --> 00:32:55.320
managing them,
ingesting device data,

00:32:55.320 --> 00:33:00.670
and sending configuration
and device updates to them.

00:33:00.670 --> 00:33:05.130
So you can see our edge solution
here, we just announced it.

00:33:05.130 --> 00:33:07.700
I'm not going to talk about it,
we don't have time for that.

00:33:07.700 --> 00:33:11.050
But please check it out
online, it's really cool.

00:33:11.050 --> 00:33:15.840
What you can see here is,
we have these end devices,

00:33:15.840 --> 00:33:18.570
and then our edge solution,
and then the cloud

00:33:18.570 --> 00:33:20.760
side of things, Cloud IoT Core.

00:33:20.760 --> 00:33:23.310
It's directly
integrated with Pub/Sub,

00:33:23.310 --> 00:33:27.165
our solution to channel
messages into our Cloud service

00:33:27.165 --> 00:33:29.070
and other cloud services.

00:33:29.070 --> 00:33:31.140
So like Cloud
Functions or Dataflow,

00:33:31.140 --> 00:33:35.130
and then to store them in
one of our storage solutions,

00:33:35.130 --> 00:33:40.610
or visualize them using
Datalab or Data Studio.

00:33:40.610 --> 00:33:41.720
The network.

00:33:41.720 --> 00:33:44.320
Very important to talk
about one of our strengths

00:33:44.320 --> 00:33:45.710
is our network.

00:33:45.710 --> 00:33:49.720
When it comes to an IoT
deployment, latency is crucial.

00:33:49.720 --> 00:33:52.450
So Google is actually
the only non ISP

00:33:52.450 --> 00:33:56.470
company owning submarine fiber.

00:33:56.470 --> 00:33:59.260
And this network that
we own enables us

00:33:59.260 --> 00:34:01.570
to provide super low latencies.

00:34:01.570 --> 00:34:03.760
And you get this out
of the box if you

00:34:03.760 --> 00:34:05.350
start using Cloud IoT Core.

00:34:09.070 --> 00:34:11.920
Just a little bit more
details about Cloud IoT Core.

00:34:11.920 --> 00:34:14.050
There are two main components.

00:34:14.050 --> 00:34:15.760
Protocol bridge.

00:34:15.760 --> 00:34:19.659
MQTT and HTTP bridges,
that's where devices connect.

00:34:19.659 --> 00:34:23.800
That's where they can send
data and receive configurations

00:34:23.800 --> 00:34:26.199
or commands.

00:34:26.199 --> 00:34:31.090
It's super scalable, and it's
directly connected to Pub/Sub.

00:34:31.090 --> 00:34:34.870
And, of course, the
scaling is automatic.

00:34:34.870 --> 00:34:39.850
The Device Manager is for
managing the devices, managing

00:34:39.850 --> 00:34:43.630
configurations, access control.

00:34:43.630 --> 00:34:48.370
And there is a nice UI as well
for managing your devices.

00:34:48.370 --> 00:34:52.340
But also, API
support is available.

00:34:52.340 --> 00:34:53.860
So security.

00:34:53.860 --> 00:34:55.670
Let's talk about
the authentication

00:34:55.670 --> 00:35:00.000
because this is really
unique in our solution.

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:04.670
So we are using a JWT
based authentication.

00:35:04.670 --> 00:35:06.960
The device has the private key.

00:35:06.960 --> 00:35:11.540
It signs a JWT token
using its private key,

00:35:11.540 --> 00:35:17.340
and overloads the password field
of the MQTT connect message

00:35:17.340 --> 00:35:19.490
with this JWT token.

00:35:19.490 --> 00:35:22.520
So essentially what
happens is that we

00:35:22.520 --> 00:35:25.820
are providing mutual
authentication

00:35:25.820 --> 00:35:30.990
without the well known TLS
mutual authentication protocol.

00:35:30.990 --> 00:35:35.570
Now, of course, this requires
the corresponding public key

00:35:35.570 --> 00:35:37.250
to be registered in the cloud.

00:35:37.250 --> 00:35:42.620
So that has to happen before the
device can authenticate itself

00:35:42.620 --> 00:35:43.820
with the cloud.

00:35:43.820 --> 00:35:48.270
And so, once again, this is
unique because you essentially

00:35:48.270 --> 00:35:50.940
get TLS mutual
authentication functionality

00:35:50.940 --> 00:35:54.820
without having to have the
full TLS stack on your device.

00:35:54.820 --> 00:35:57.430
So that can save
resources on your devices,

00:35:57.430 --> 00:36:00.592
which is a usual problem
in case of resource

00:36:00.592 --> 00:36:01.425
constrained devices.

00:36:04.470 --> 00:36:04.980
All right.

00:36:04.980 --> 00:36:09.930
So let's take a look at
the basic provisioning

00:36:09.930 --> 00:36:12.430
flow, how you register devices.

00:36:12.430 --> 00:36:15.810
So the provisioner,
whoever the provisioner is,

00:36:15.810 --> 00:36:22.230
creates a key pair, stores
the private key in the device,

00:36:22.230 --> 00:36:24.550
and then registers the
public key together

00:36:24.550 --> 00:36:27.660
with the device ID in the cloud.

00:36:27.660 --> 00:36:32.430
And then the device can
connect by signing a JWT using

00:36:32.430 --> 00:36:36.470
its private key to the cloud.

00:36:36.470 --> 00:36:39.740
Now, this looks simple
and good, but this is not

00:36:39.740 --> 00:36:41.690
the most secure solution.

00:36:41.690 --> 00:36:47.480
And that's because the key pairs
are generated using software.

00:36:47.480 --> 00:36:51.620
And we just discussed
that using a secure chip

00:36:51.620 --> 00:36:55.920
is more like a highly secure
solution, more secure solution.

00:36:55.920 --> 00:36:59.660
So this is how the flow looks
like using the secure chip.

00:36:59.660 --> 00:37:03.810
So the difference is
really that the secure chip

00:37:03.810 --> 00:37:05.000
is added to your device.

00:37:05.000 --> 00:37:07.280
It already contains
the public keys.

00:37:07.280 --> 00:37:11.840
And whoever the OEM is, whoever
manufactures the devices,

00:37:11.840 --> 00:37:14.990
receives the public
key as a file,

00:37:14.990 --> 00:37:21.320
and can run a script to
register these devices.

00:37:21.320 --> 00:37:24.830
And then it's the same
process when it comes

00:37:24.830 --> 00:37:27.080
to the connection of devices.

00:37:27.080 --> 00:37:29.120
Now, this is highly secure.

00:37:29.120 --> 00:37:29.900
It's simple.

00:37:29.900 --> 00:37:32.150
It all looks good.

00:37:32.150 --> 00:37:34.040
But is this enough?

00:37:34.040 --> 00:37:34.810
No.

00:37:34.810 --> 00:37:38.660
So there are still
challenges here.

00:37:38.660 --> 00:37:41.030
So there are two main
areas of challenges.

00:37:41.030 --> 00:37:43.270
One is key management.

00:37:43.270 --> 00:37:45.640
How do you secure the
private keys in case

00:37:45.640 --> 00:37:46.880
you do not use a secure chip?

00:37:46.880 --> 00:37:49.380
Because not everyone
is using a secure chip.

00:37:49.380 --> 00:37:51.640
But even if you
use a secure chip,

00:37:51.640 --> 00:37:55.270
how do you transfer the public
keys from the secure element

00:37:55.270 --> 00:37:59.180
provider, like
Microchip, to the OEM,

00:37:59.180 --> 00:38:00.830
to the device manufacturer.

00:38:00.830 --> 00:38:06.260
The other big area of
challenges is device management.

00:38:06.260 --> 00:38:12.810
How do you map the public keys
to projects or registries?

00:38:12.810 --> 00:38:18.370
So how do you define the
location of your device

00:38:18.370 --> 00:38:20.090
in your cloud project?

00:38:20.090 --> 00:38:23.780
And these decisions
have to be made

00:38:23.780 --> 00:38:26.010
at the time of manufacturing.

00:38:26.010 --> 00:38:28.880
So overall, it's
a complex project.

00:38:28.880 --> 00:38:33.140
And to tackle these
two problem spaces,

00:38:33.140 --> 00:38:35.300
we are happy to
introduce Cloud IoT

00:38:35.300 --> 00:38:40.100
Provisioning, a new
service, available as part

00:38:40.100 --> 00:38:41.270
of an early access program.

00:38:41.270 --> 00:38:43.700
So you can apply for it.

00:38:43.700 --> 00:38:46.910
And it's essentially
an interface

00:38:46.910 --> 00:38:49.580
connecting secure
element providers

00:38:49.580 --> 00:38:51.890
and device manufacturers, OEMs.

00:38:51.890 --> 00:38:56.240
So secure element providers
can upload public key data

00:38:56.240 --> 00:38:58.990
into the cloud directly.

00:38:58.990 --> 00:39:06.120
And this public
key data together

00:39:06.120 --> 00:39:08.230
with the corresponding
secure element

00:39:08.230 --> 00:39:11.080
ID is available for the
device manufacturers.

00:39:11.080 --> 00:39:14.470
So the device manufacturers
can create and assign

00:39:14.470 --> 00:39:17.980
targets for these
secure elements.

00:39:17.980 --> 00:39:20.680
And, probably most
importantly, it

00:39:20.680 --> 00:39:26.650
provides a way to create generic
devices, devices for which you

00:39:26.650 --> 00:39:29.890
don't need to set the target
information, the project

00:39:29.890 --> 00:39:33.100
registry information, at
the time of manufacturing.

00:39:33.100 --> 00:39:36.760
They can download
this configuration

00:39:36.760 --> 00:39:39.330
as part of the first boot.

00:39:39.330 --> 00:39:41.680
So let's see a
demonstration of this.

00:39:55.180 --> 00:40:01.780
All right, this is the Cloud
IoT Provisioning early version

00:40:01.780 --> 00:40:03.190
interface.

00:40:03.190 --> 00:40:06.840
As I mentioned, there
are two personas here,

00:40:06.840 --> 00:40:10.510
the SE manufacturer, so the
secure element provider,

00:40:10.510 --> 00:40:12.160
and the OEM.

00:40:12.160 --> 00:40:15.280
And in this case, the
secure element data

00:40:15.280 --> 00:40:16.940
